    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's what I implemented to satisfy the \"reject a future registrationDate with 400\" rule.\n\n## Changes\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (no `@RestController`, errors via escalation handlers):\n\n1. **`RejectFutureRegistrationDate.java`** (new function) \u2014 reads the effective `Owner` via `@Val`, and throws a checked `InvalidRegistrationDateException` when `registrationDate.isAfter(LocalDate.now())`. It runs **before** `NormalizeOwnerRegistrationDate` so the check is on the *supplied* date, not a weekend-rolled one. Since `BuildOwner` defaults an absent date to the server date (never in the future), only a genuinely future supplied date is rejected.\n\n2. **`InvalidRegistrationDateException.java`** (new) \u2014 a checked exception, mirroring the existing `InvalidTelephoneException` etc.\n\n3. **`InvalidRegistrationDateExceptionHandler.java`** (new) \u2014 responds **400** with a `ProblemDetail`, matching the pattern of the other 400 handlers.\n\n4. **Escalation YAML** `...InvalidRegistrationDateException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `rejectFutureRegistrationDate` step (governed by `transaction`) between `normalizeAddress` and `normalizeRegistrationDate`.\n\n## Test result\n- `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsFutureDate` (posts `registrationDate: \"2999-01-01\"`, expects 400) passes.\n- Full suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
