        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots cleanly into the established conventions of this codebase \u2014 no structural concerns.\n\n- **Right unit, right pattern.** `RejectFutureRegistrationDate` mirrors the existing sibling reject-functions (`RejectCityAtCapacity`, `RejectOverDailyLimit`) exactly: one public `service(@Val Owner ...)` method, throws a checked escalation, carries a javadoc that names where it runs in the pipeline and why. It is one small single-responsibility function class, not an addition to an existing catch-all.\n- **Handler/exception are idiomatic.** `InvalidRegistrationDateException` is a copy-shaped twin of `InvalidPostcodeException`/`InvalidAddressException`, and `InvalidRegistrationDateExceptionHandler` is byte-for-byte consistent with the other 400 handlers, correctly reusing the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper rather than re-implementing the ProblemDetail assembly. No duplication introduced \u2014 it reuses the one factory every handler uses.\n- **Pipeline wiring is consistent.** The new step is inserted with the same `govern: [ transaction ]` as every surrounding step and threaded via `next:` with no orphaned/rewired branches. Placement before `normalizeRegistrationDate` is a deliberate, documented choice (check the supplied date, not the weekend-rolled one), and both the YAML comment and the class javadoc explain it coherently.\n- **Escalation YAML** follows the `<fully.qualified.Exception>.yml` \u2192 single `handle:` convention used by every other escalation file.\n\nLogic is in the right place, DTOs stay at the edges (entity-level check in the middle), and a maintainer would recognize this as the house style."
